# ASUS ROG Phone 5/5s (ASUS_I005D/ASUS_I005_1) hardware fixups.
#
# ASUS's own vendor/etc/init/hw/init.asus.rc chowns/relabels the touch IC's
# rise_report_rate and edge_settings attributes to system:system +
# vendor_sysfs_touch (confirmed live via ls -laZ), letting system_app write
# them directly - but it doesn't do the same for fts_game_mode, an apparent
# gap in ASUS's own script. Since that's on /vendor and can't be patched
# from here, this compensates for it on the /system side instead, matching
# the same target ownership/mode ASUS uses for its sibling attributes.
# Paired with the genfs_contexts relabel to vendor_sysfs_touch (same
# sepolicy/genfs_contexts) - DAC alone isn't enough, both are needed for
# system_app to actually be able to write it.
#
# Verified live: the sepolicy half of this fix landed and correctly
# relabels fts_game_mode to vendor_sysfs_touch, but DAC ownership was still
# root:root after boot - this file was present and its content correct, so
# the chown/chmod actions just never actually applied. This touch IC's i2c
# bus (990000.i2c) shows real GSI QN/xfer-timeout errors in dmesg well past
# early boot on this unit, so the "on boot" trigger (which fires before
# many late-probing peripherals finish) most likely ran before this
# specific sysfs attribute existed - chown/chmod on a path that doesn't
# exist yet silently no-ops. Moved to sys.boot_completed=1, the latest
# point in the standard boot trigger sequence, to guarantee the node
# exists by the time this runs.
on property:sys.boot_completed=1
    chown system system /sys/devices/platform/soc/990000.i2c/i2c-2/2-0038/fts_game_mode
    chmod 0664 /sys/devices/platform/soc/990000.i2c/i2c-2/2-0038/fts_game_mode
    restorecon /sys/devices/platform/soc/990000.i2c/i2c-2/2-0038/fts_game_mode
